                    briefing_text_r2:       `Build the operating model that restores adaptive performance — inside a <strong>hard 6 engineer-hours / person / week</strong> for all non-coding learning activity. Choose a coherent bundle of channels (peer feedback, redesigned retro, individual after-action review, anonymous upward feedback, customer loop) and decide how reflection feeds the next sprint. Remember: <em>feedback without reflection is data; feedback plus reflection that changes the next attempt is learning.</em> If Tuesday's reflection doesn't change Wednesday's sprint, you've built a journal, not a loop. Deliverable: a feedback-and-reflection model with the time budget reconciled and the loop closed.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Re-tune PulseHR under a trust constraint`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The board will <strong>not</strong> let you switch PulseHR off — it cost A$1.1M and runs company-wide. Re-tune it under one rule: every change must <em>raise</em> the “I trust how I'm evaluated” score, not just optimise output. Down-weight the signals driving the gaming; decide individual vs squad-level scoring; and draw the boundary that broke Atlas — <strong>developmental vs evaluative</strong>. While one number feeds both growth and pay, admitting a weakness costs money, so candour stays suppressed. A facilitator-played senior engineer reacts to each move. Deliverable: a revised PulseHR config with a trust rationale per change.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Recover & institutionalise`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Pitch a 90-day recovery plan to the VP Engineering. Sequence it with <strong>leading</strong> indicators (feedback-seeking events, trust score — they move in week 3) and <strong>lagging</strong> ones (rework rate, on-time delivery). State the <em>stop condition</em> that tells you the redesign is failing before the delivery numbers move. Then decide what scales to the other 21 squads — and how. Mandating the Atlas design company-wide next Monday is <strong>exactly how PulseHR was born</strong>: one good idea, scaled by fiat. Pilot, measure the leading indicators, adapt, then scale what travels. Deliverable: a 90-day recovery & scale-out plan with a leading/lagging dashboard.`,

