                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_imagecost:     `<strong>That's the cost that matters.</strong> Anseel's research is clear: feedback-seeking falls when the image cost of admitting a weakness is high — and piping weekly scores into pay made every “how am I doing?” a confession that hurts your calibration. You changed the cost, not the count. Now fix the environment, not just the dashboard.`,
                    coach_r1_channels:      `<strong>“Not enough channels” is the count, not the cost.</strong> We had channels — we stopped using them because using them is expensive to our scores. Open more and you've added more places to feel judged. What did you change about the cost of candour?`,
                    coach_r1_metrics:       `<strong>Debugging the dashboards misses it.</strong> The metrics aren't buggy — they're working exactly as designed, and that's the problem. People optimise what's measured. The issue isn't measurement accuracy, it's what the measurement does to the cost of being honest.`,
                    coach_r1_lazy:          `<strong>“The engineers got lazy” is the diagnosis that guarantees you fail.</strong> These were Lumera's best squad a year ago. Nobody got lazy — they got rational. They protect their PulseHR scores because those scores set their pay. Blame the system, not the people, or you'll re-create it.`,
                    coach_r1_same:          `<strong>They are not the same thing.</strong> A precise measurement system can be a terrible feedback environment — that's exactly what happened here. Conflate them and you'll “fix” the metric and wonder why candour still doesn't return.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You separated the feedback environment from the measurement system AND named the image cost the algorithm inflated. That's the lens that tells you the fix is about the cost of candour, not the number of channels — exactly where the interventions have to land.`,
                    coach_r1_overconfident: `<strong>That's a lot of conviction on a shallow read.</strong> You haven't named the real cost or separated the two systems, yet you're ready to charge in. Over-confidence on the wrong diagnosis is how you spend the quarter fixing the wrong thing. Slow down and re-diagnose.`,
                    coach_r1_stalled:       `<strong>You can't diagnose forever.</strong> Atlas has a 10-week window and a 3rd miss already on the board. You need enough conviction to act — endless analysis with no hypothesis is its own failure mode here.`,
                    coach_r2_close:         `<strong>That's a closed loop.</strong> One concrete change committed to the very next sprint is what turns reflection into learning — Anseel's reflection work only lifts performance when the reflection feeds the next attempt. The arrow closes; now keep it inside the time budget.`,
                    coach_r2_journal:       `<strong>You've built a journal, not a learning loop.</strong> Individual after-action reviews and paired reflection are good — but if Tuesday's insight doesn't change Wednesday's sprint, where does it go? Show me the feedback arrow that closes, or this is paperwork.`,
                    coach_r2_report:        `<strong>A quarterly report is too slow to be a loop.</strong> Reflection improves the next attempt only if it reaches the next sprint, not the next calibration. Connect each reflection to a concrete change in the very next sprint, not a document the lead reads in three months.`,
                    coach_r2_volume:        `<strong>You've opened a lot of channels and never closed the loop.</strong> More touchpoints isn't more feedback culture — Anseel says seeking falls when image cost is high, regardless of how many channels you open. You've added places to feel judged without changing the cost or feeding reflection back into the work.`,
                    coach_r2_overload:      `<strong>Add it up — that's too much.</strong> Peer feedback, redesigned retro, written reflection, upward feedback, customer loops: you're drowning a squad that's already missing sprints. The learning system competes with delivery for the scarcest resource — engineer time. What gets cut to fit six hours, and why is that the right thing to drop?`,
                    coach_r2_thin:          `<strong>That's a very thin design.</strong> A couple of hours spread across everything won't shift adaptive performance. You have room inside the six-hour budget — invest enough in the routines that actually close the loop (the retro and the after-action review).`,
                    coach_r3_tune:          `<strong>Down-weighting the gamed signals is the surgical move.</strong> Velocity and review-latency are exactly what people learned to optimise instead of the underlying goal — a Goodhart failure. Soften those and keep peer-recognition light, and you've cut the gaming without detonating a A$1.1M platform the board just funded.`,
                    coach_r3_keepall:       `<strong>Keeping all three at full weight keeps the gaming.</strong> “Objectivity above all” is how Atlas got here — when the measure becomes the goal, people optimise the measure. You haven't changed anything that drove the behaviour; the trust score won't move.`,
                    coach_r3_addmore:       `<strong>More signals is more surface to game.</strong> The CFO's break-even narrative wants granularity, but every new tracked metric is another number to protect and another reason not to be candid. You're scaling the exact failure, not fixing it.`,
                    coach_r3_detonate:      `<strong>You've recommended scrapping a A$1.1M company-wide platform the board just funded.</strong> Walk into that board meeting and defend it. The answer isn't off or on — it's re-tuned. Which two signals drive the gaming, and what happens if you down-weight those instead of detonating the whole system?`,
                    coach_r3_split:         `<strong>That's the boundary that rebuilds trust.</strong> Using PulseHR for development only — with calibration on separate, human-reviewed evidence — means admitting a weakness no longer costs me money. That, plus down-weighting the gamed signals, is what lets me ask “how am I doing?” again without it setting my pay.`,
                    coach_r3_payonly:       `<strong>Pay-only is honest about what it is, but it doesn't restore candour.</strong> If the number still sets my pay, I still won't disclose a weakness to it. You've removed the developmental pretence — but the cost of candour is unchanged.`,
                    coach_r3_both:          `<strong>One number for both is what broke Atlas.</strong> Tell me PulseHR still pipes weekly scores straight into pay calibration, then ask me to admit in a retro that I'm struggling. I won't — admitting weakness now costs me money. You can use it to develop or to judge; using one score for both keeps candour expensive.`,
                    coach_r3_opaque:        `<strong>Opaque scoring breeds distrust.</strong> If I can't see or appeal my own data, the algorithm stays a black box that judges me. Transparency and an appeal route are part of the trust rebuild, not a nice-to-have.`,
                    coach_r4_leading:       `<strong>Leading indicators are the early-warning system.</strong> Feedback-seeking events and the trust score move in week 3 — long before rework and on-time delivery do. Anchor the dashboard on those and you'll know the redesign is working before the delivery numbers confirm it.`,
                    coach_r4_ontime:        `<strong>On-time delivery is a lagging indicator.</strong> By the time it moves, you're already late or already recovered — it can't warn you in week 3. Pair it with a leading indicator, or you're flying blind through the window that matters.`,
                    coach_r4_velocity:      `<strong>Velocity is the number that started the gaming.</strong> Anchoring the recovery dashboard on it tells the squad you're still watching the metric they learned to protect. Lead with feedback-seeking and trust instead.`,
                    coach_r4_nostop:        `<strong>No stop condition is how good ideas calcify into the next PulseHR.</strong> Without a leading-indicator threshold that says “pause and re-diagnose,” you'll push a failing redesign straight through to scale. Define what tells you to stop before delivery does.`,
                    coach_r4_pilot:         `<strong>Pilot, measure, adapt, then scale — that's the lesson.</strong> Mandating the Atlas fix across 22 squads next Monday is precisely how PulseHR was born: one good idea, scaled by fiat. Prove it on two or three volunteer squads against the leading indicators first, and scale only what travels.`,
                    coach_r4_freeze:        `<strong>Atlas-only forever wastes what you learned.</strong> The point isn't to hoard the fix — it's to scale it responsibly. Pilot the design elsewhere, measure, and let the leading indicators tell you what generalises.`,
                    coach_r4_fiat:          `<strong>You loved the Atlas design so much you've mandated it company-wide next Monday.</strong> That is exactly how PulseHR was born — one good idea, scaled by fiat, with no pilot and no measurement. You're about to re-create the failure you just repaired, at 22× the scale.`,

